  "transcript": "# Work of the Department for Energy Security and Net Zero\n\n## Progress on Clean Power 2030\n> Ministers say the UK has unlocked large clean power investment and made progress but delivery faces network and planning bottlenecks.\n\nChair: How well are we doing on the Clean Power 2030 target?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: The target has built momentum. We've unlocked around £100bn of investment, consented capacity for over 20 million homes, and renewables supplied the system two-thirds of the time last year. Progress is real, but more work is needed.\nChair: Developers tell us projects are blocked by grid gates: solar‑plus‑storage get stuck because they sit in different queue stages. Does the system need an overhaul?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: Parts of the system share the same ambition but struggle because of historic underinvestment in networks. We reordered queues, launched Great British Grid to accelerate build‑out, and are working with operators on workforce and practical barriers.\nJonathan Brearley: Ofgem, NESO and DESNZ want the same outcome. The issue is scale and complexity: planning resource, grid queue ordering and execution need to speed up. We're stabilising connections but it's a big job.\n\n\n## Regulator independence vs policy delivery\n> Government recognises tensions between regulator independence and delivering strategic policy, especially for cost‑effective long‑duration storage.\n\nChair: Some allege Ofgem favours up‑front cost over lifetime value, excluding longer‑term solutions. Does that conflict with ministers' policy?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: We hear those frustrations and want change. We back innovation and schemes that support long‑duration storage; where companies challenge outcomes, Ofgem has consulted and will respond.\nJonathan Brearley: Regulator independence means Ofgem implements policy. Government is firming up policy statements so the regulator and industry know what to deliver; some high‑CAPEX, long‑benefit projects have already passed mind‑to decisions.\nChair: Are you committed to using CfDs for solar?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: CfDs have been effective in unlocking investment. We'll keep using them where they make sense while balancing incentives against consumer costs.\n\n\n## Affordability and decoupling from gas\n> Ministers say home‑grown clean power is essential to reduce exposure to volatile global gas prices, but system reform and network build‑out are also needed.\n\nMelanie Onn: With UK prices among the highest in Europe, what is the plan to bring energy costs down?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: Affordability is the top priority. Short‑term relief includes VAT removal and targeted support, but long‑term we must deploy home‑grown clean energy, reform market structure, and invest in critical infrastructure and fair cost allocation.\nMelanie Onn: If grid build‑out lags, can we decouple electricity prices from international gas?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: Decoupling is under way: more renewables on the system reduces gas influence and CfDs fix renewable prices. But network constraints remain and must be tackled so renewables can connect and displace gas.\nMelanie Onn: Is Government funding for networks sufficient?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: We are funding key parts and using recovery mechanisms, but industry flags workforce and practical constraints more than lack of funding. We will work in step with industry to resolve those barriers.\n\n\n## Warm Homes, heat technologies and local delivery\n> Government will keep funding warm homes programmes and widen technology support, but delivery needs local capability and clearer standards.\n\nWera Hobhouse: You are adding heat batteries. Why are other proven options like electric boilers excluded from grants and standards?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: We judge technologies by ease of adoption, emissions impact and need for subsidy. The Boiler Upgrade Scheme has broadened over time; we're open to reviewing other technologies and will look into the company you mentioned.\nJonathan Brearley: We work closely with BSI on standards. Criteria exist to ensure public money is used properly, but we're ready to adjust as technology changes and invite innovators to engage with the innovation team.\nLizzi Collinge: Warm Homes grants show postcode variation and local authorities with no delivery capacity struggle because grants are paid in arrears. Will devolution help?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: Devolution should help by enabling area‑based upgrades and blending funds. We'll establish a Warm Homes agency in shadow form to support places lacking capacity and evolve the scheme with local feedback.\nLizzi Collinge: Will the zero VAT on solar materials be extended?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: We have asked the Chancellor but tax matters are his decision.\n\n\n## Nuclear regulation, sites and the Fingleton agenda\n> The Department is implementing Fingleton recommendations, pushing legislation where needed, and seeking clearer regulatory rules to balance safety with strategic energy needs.\n\nLizzi Collinge: Old planning criteria prevent some new nuclear sites. When will those criteria and Fingleton recommendations be implemented?\nJonathan Brearley: We're working through all 47 Fingleton recommendations. Some need legislation; others don't. We will provide a written update on progress and timings.\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: We want a swift Bill but need parliamentary time. We recognise industry urgency and are pressing for a slot; some reform can be done without legislation and is already happening.\nLizzi Collinge: Should the Office for Nuclear Regulation be required to weigh strategic objectives—like energy security—into safety decisions?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: Policy intent is that strategic factors are considered. We will look at the most appropriate mechanism to ensure they feed into regulatory decision‑making.\nClaire Young: What role will GB Energy‑North and GBE‑N sites play, and is their engagement with local authorities clear?\nJonathan Brearley: GBE‑N are identifying potential sites; we will follow up on transparency and engagement concerns. ANF work and site allocation are being coordinated with industry.\n\n\n## Security, resilience and accountability\n> Ministers said the UK has robust contingency arrangements for physical and cyber threats, will continue testing, and that DESNZ must lead strategic coordination across bodies.\n\nTorcuil Crichton: Can you assure consumers the lights won't go out this winter?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: I'm 100% confident we will not have national outages this winter. We have tested the system and have diverse supply and contingency plans.\nGraeme Downie: With recent attacks on energy infrastructure abroad, who decides and acts if something happens to offshore assets here?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: We have a cross‑government framework including the security services; serious incidents would go to COBRA. If companies feel unclear who to call, they should come to us.\nGraeme Downie: Are cyber and AI‑driven threats being factored into plans?\nJonathan Brearley: Yes. We work with NCSC, Ofgem and others; new legislation will broaden powers and supply‑chain oversight. Resilience work is ongoing and must evolve with the threat.\nChair: Who is ultimately accountable for fixing the coordination between DESNZ, Ofgem, NESO and others?\nMiatta Fahnbulleh: Ultimately me. We accept the system is noisy; DESNZ will set clearer strategic direction and strengthen coordination. GB Grid and other reforms aim to simplify delivery.\n",
